    QuoteModel(
      id: 'csv_2771',
      authorEn: 'Martin Heidegger', authorPt: 'Martin Heidegger', authorEs: 'Martin Heidegger',
      textEn: 'Ontically, of course, Dasein is not only close to us\u2015even that which is closest: we *are* it, each of us, we ourselves. In spite of this, or rather for just this reason, it is ontologically that which is farthest. To be sure, its ownmost Being is such that it has an understanding of that Being, and already maintains itself in each case as if its Being has been interpreted in some manner. But we are certainly not saying that when Dasein\'s own Being is thus interpreted pre-ontologically in the way which lies closest, this interpretation can be taken over as an appropriate clue, as if this way of understanding Being is what must emerge when one\'s ownmost state of Being is considered as an ontological theme. The kind of Being which belongs to Dasein is rather such that, in understanding its own Being, it has a tendency to do so in terms of that entity towards which it comports itself proximally and in a way which is essentially constant\u2015in terms of the \'world\'. In Dasein itself, and therefore in its own understanding of Being, the way the world is understood is, as we shall show, reflected back ontologically upon the way in which Dasein itself gets interpreted.Thus because Dasein is ontico-ontologically prior, its own specific state of Being (if we understand this in the sense of Dasein\'s \'categorial structure\') remains concealed from it. Dasein is ontically \'closest\' to itself and ontologically farthest; but pre-ontologically it is surely not a stranger."\u2015from_Being and Time_. Translated by John Macquarrie & Edward Robinson, pp. 36-37',
      textPt: 'Onticamente, \u00e9 claro, o Dasein n\u00e3o est\u00e1 apenas pr\u00f3ximo de n\u00f3s \u2014 \u00e9 mesmo o mais pr\u00f3ximo: n\u00f3s o somos, cada um de n\u00f3s, n\u00f3s mesmos. Apesar disso, ou justamente por isso, ontologicamente \u00e9 o que est\u00e1 mais distante. Decerto, seu Ser mais pr\u00f3prio \u00e9 tal que tem uma compreens\u00e3o desse Ser, e em cada caso j\u00e1 se mant\u00e9m como se seu Ser estivesse interpretado de alguma maneira. Mas certamente n\u00e3o dizemos que, quando o pr\u00f3prio Ser do Dasein \u00e9 interpretado pr\u00e9-ontologicamente do modo mais pr\u00f3ximo, essa interpreta\u00e7\u00e3o possa ser tomada como pista adequada... \u2014 de_Ser e Tempo_, traduzido por J. Macquarrie & E. Robinson, pp. 36-37',
      textEs: '\u00d3nticamente, por supuesto, el Dasein no solo est\u00e1 cerca de nosotros \u2014 es lo m\u00e1s cercano: lo *somos*, cada uno de nosotros, nosotros mismos. A pesar de esto, o m\u00e1s bien precisamente por esto, ontol\u00f3gicamente es lo m\u00e1s lejano. Sin duda, su Ser m\u00e1s propio es tal que tiene una comprensi\u00f3n de ese Ser, y ya se mantiene en cada caso como si su Ser hubiera sido interpretado de alguna manera. Pero no estamos diciendo que cuando el propio Ser del Dasein se interpreta as\u00ed preontol\u00f3gicamente del modo m\u00e1s cercano, esa interpretaci\u00f3n pueda tomarse como pista apropiada... \u2014 de_Ser y Tiempo_, traducido por J. Macquarrie y E. Robinson, pp. 36-37',
